WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.668
On Linux, every file and every process belongs to a user.

2
00:00:06.668 --> 00:00:10.009
A process carries the ID of the user it runs as.

3
00:00:10.233 --> 00:00:16.171
When the process opens a file, the kernel compares that user with the file's owner and permissions.

4
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:21.527
Only root, the superuser with user ID zero, skips almost every check.

5
00:00:22.333 --> 00:00:25.455
To see a file's permissions, run ls -l.

6
00:00:25.667 --> 00:00:27.783
Look at the first ten characters.

7
00:00:27.783 --> 00:00:32.784
The first one is the type: a dash for a file, d for a directory, l for a link.

8
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.273
The next nine are three groups of three.

9
00:00:35.273 --> 00:00:40.501
What the owner may do, what the group may do, and what everyone else, called others, may do.

10
00:00:40.733 --> 00:00:45.007
Then come the links, the owner and group, the size, the time, and the name.

11
00:00:45.267 --> 00:00:50.746
In each triplet, r is read, w is write, and x is execute, which means run.

12
00:00:50.746 --> 00:00:52.597
A dash means not allowed.

13
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.405
Now change it with chmod 750.

14
00:00:55.405 --> 00:00:57.741
Others get nothing at all.

15
00:00:57.967 --> 00:00:59.880
Say user dev tries to run it.

16
00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:03.377
For each check, the kernel picks exactly one triplet.

17
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:05.668
Owner, if you own the file.

18
00:01:05.668 --> 00:01:06.664
dev does not.

19
00:01:06.867 --> 00:01:09.249
Else group, if you are in its group.

20
00:01:09.249 --> 00:01:11.168
dev is not in the group root.

21
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:12.307
Else, others.

22
00:01:12.307 --> 00:01:16.704
So for dev, only the last three count, and they are all dashes.

23
00:01:16.933 --> 00:01:18.128
Permission denied.

24
00:01:18.128 --> 00:01:22.707
The first triplet that fits decides, even if a later one allows more.

25
00:01:23.533 --> 00:01:25.760
chmod also takes numbers.

26
00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:28.787
r is worth four, w two, and x one.

27
00:01:28.787 --> 00:01:30.657
A dash is worth zero.

28
00:01:30.867 --> 00:01:33.403
Add them up for each triplet.

29
00:01:33.403 --> 00:01:37.950
rwx is seven, r-x is five, and three dashes is zero.

30
00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:40.487
That is where 750 comes from.

31
00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:43.447
You will see three all the time.

32
00:01:43.447 --> 00:01:50.057
755 for scripts and folders, 644 for files, and 600, owner only, for secrets.

33
00:01:50.967 --> 00:01:53.066
A program is a file on disk.

34
00:01:53.066 --> 00:01:59.513
A process is one running copy of it, and the kernel gives each process an ID, its PID.

35
00:01:59.733 --> 00:02:03.201
Here is nginx, from the lesson's container.

36
00:02:03.201 --> 00:02:06.829
One master process runs as root, with PID 98.

37
00:02:07.033 --> 00:02:10.941
It starts four workers, as the user www-data.

38
00:02:10.941 --> 00:02:14.068
Their PPID, the parent's PID, is 98.

39
00:02:14.068 --> 00:02:16.760
They are the master's children.

40
00:02:16.967 --> 00:02:20.771
So the code that answers strangers cannot touch root's files.

41
00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:27.478
To stop a process, you send it a signal, a short message the kernel delivers.

42
00:02:27.478 --> 00:02:28.604
Meet worker.sh.

43
00:02:28.833 --> 00:02:32.851
Every two seconds, it processes one job, and prints a line.

44
00:02:33.067 --> 00:02:34.686
Run kill with its PID.

45
00:02:34.686 --> 00:02:39.544
That sends SIGTERM, signal fifteen: a polite request to shut down.

46
00:02:39.767 --> 00:02:41.973
worker.sh catches it with trap.

47
00:02:41.973 --> 00:02:47.311
It finishes the job it is on, closes up, and exits cleanly, with code zero.

48
00:02:47.533 --> 00:02:48.936
Start it again.

49
00:02:48.936 --> 00:02:51.088
This time, run kill -9.

50
00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.497
That sends SIGKILL, signal nine.

51
00:02:53.497 --> 00:02:56.519
It cannot be caught, so the trap never runs.

52
00:02:56.733 --> 00:02:59.750
The process ends at once, and no cleanup runs.

53
00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:02.571
The log just stops, in the middle of a job.

54
00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:09.559
When a signal ends a process, its exit code is 128 plus the signal number.

55
00:03:09.767 --> 00:03:18.243
So 137 means SIGKILL, 143 means SIGTERM, and 130 means someone pressed Ctrl+C.

56
00:03:18.467 --> 00:03:20.472
Containers get the same pair.

57
00:03:20.472 --> 00:03:24.965
docker stop sends SIGTERM, waits ten seconds, then sends SIGKILL.

58
00:03:25.167 --> 00:03:28.993
Kubernetes does the same, with a thirty second default.

59
00:03:28.993 --> 00:03:33.863
So send SIGTERM first, and keep kill -9 for a process that ignores it.

60
00:03:34.767 --> 00:03:39.098
Who starts the worker again after a reboot, or a crash at 3 am?

61
00:03:39.098 --> 00:03:42.055
On almost every Linux server, systemd does.

62
00:03:42.267 --> 00:03:46.988
It runs each service from a unit file, like this hello-worker.service.

63
00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:49.575
ExecStart is the command to run.

64
00:03:49.575 --> 00:03:53.955
User says to run it as the normal ubuntu user, not as root.

65
00:03:54.167 --> 00:03:59.421
If the worker crashes, Restart=on-failure tells systemd to start it again.

66
00:03:59.633 --> 00:04:03.224
WantedBy=multi-user.target means: start it at boot.

67
00:04:03.224 --> 00:04:06.111
You switch that on with systemctl enable.

68
00:04:06.333 --> 00:04:11.823
Everything the worker prints goes into systemd's journal, and journalctl reads it back.

69
00:04:12.033 --> 00:04:18.248
And stopping the service sends SIGTERM to worker.sh first, so it can finish the job it is on.

70
00:04:18.467 --> 00:04:21.522
Permission bits decide who may touch a file.

71
00:04:21.522 --> 00:04:24.370
Signals ask, or force, a process to stop.

72
00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:27.148
And systemd keeps your services running.
